  "statement": {
    "case_for": "B012 joins a lucid mechanistic vaccine principle to exceptionally mature preventive benefit against infection and cancer. Its long-standing efficacy, public-health significance, and coherent three-person attribution make it the most complete achievement on the shortlist. The proposed laureates provide a defensible bounded account of the foundational VLP vaccine work.",
    "case_against": "The achievement rests on wider contributions in papillomavirus biology, clinical trials, manufacturing, and implementation than three names can capture. Its preventive effect also depends on uneven uptake, while population-level cancer-incidence effects accrue over different timeframes.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "perlmann-thomas",
        "point": "B014 is placed first because GLP-1 physiology both revealed a major regulatory system and enabled highly consequential diabetes and obesity therapies.",
        "response": "I agree that GLP-1 is an exceptionally strong alternative with established clinical impact. However, the boundary between foundational physiology, pharmacology, and industrial drug development leaves a more difficult three-person attribution than the focused VLP vaccine achievement."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "wahren-herlenius-marie",
        "point": "B042 is placed first because cGAS-STING established a major cytosolic-DNA sensing mechanism with broad implications across disease contexts.",
        "response": "The pathway is a compelling fundamental discovery, but the supplied record identifies substantial attribution pressure among five credited scientists and less even clinical consequences from pathway manipulation. These qualifications place it below the directly demonstrated preventive achievement of B012."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the boundary of B012 should emphasize foundational VLP work alone or include the broader vaccine-development chain remains unresolved.",
      "The appropriate allocation of credit among the three named investigators and other major contributors requires continued historical scrutiny."
    ],
